BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Preservation Hall Jazz Band: Creole Christmas
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261218T193000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261218T211500
UID:992591687630bc834a0b14ac52378cd8@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:Get in the holiday spirit with a New Orleans twist!\nThe Prese
 rvation Hall Jazz Band brings their beloved Creole Christmas to Bloomingto
 n! Join as the band performs New Orleans classics as well as some of our b
 est-loved holiday selections with a twist.\nThe group derives its name fro
 m Preservation Hall\, the venerable music venue located in the heart of Ne
 w Orleans’ French Quarter\, founded in 1961 by Allan and Sandra Jaffe. T
 hey’ve traveled worldwide spreading their mission to nurture and perpetu
 ate the art form of New Orleans Jazz. Whether performing at Carnegie Hall 
 or Lincoln Center\, for British Royalty or the King of Thailand\, this mus
 ic embodies a joyful\, timeless spirit. Under the auspices of current dire
 ctor\, Ben Jaffe\, the son of founders Allan and Sandra\, Preservation Hal
 l continues with a deep reverence and consciousness of its greatest attrib
 utes in the modern day as a venue\, band\, and record label.\nThe PHJB beg
 an touring in 1963 and for many years there were several bands successfull
 y touring under the name Preservation Hall. Many of the band’s charter m
 embers performed with the pioneers who invented jazz in the early twentiet
 h century including Buddy Bolden\, Jelly Roll Morton\, Louis Armstrong\, a
 nd Bunk Johnson. Band leaders over the band’s history include the brothe
 rs Willie and Percy Humphrey\, husband and wife Billie and De De Pierce\, 
 famed pianist Sweet Emma Barrett\, and in the modern day Wendall and John 
 Brunious. These founding artists and dozens of others passed on the lesson
 s of their music to a younger generation who now follow in their footsteps
  like the current lineup.
GEO:39.1662844;-86.5328085
LOCATION:Buskirk-Chumley Theater\, 114 E. Kirkwood Avenue\, Bloomington\, 
 IN 47408
URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/event/preservation-hall-jazz-band-cre
 ole-christmas/60064/
X-SOURCE:Visit Bloomington
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/events/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Treasures of the Silk Road
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261218T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261218T180000
UID:3b311a2985c2d8ebcd8f39f8bd6fb3f4@visitbloomington.com
DESCRIPTION:The McCalla Center\, in collaboration with musician and schola
 r Shahyar Daneshgar\, celebrates the opening of its new exhibition Treasur
 es of the Silk Road on Friday\, September 25th\, 4:00 pm-6:00 pm EDT.\nThe
  exhibit features over 70 musical instruments from the Pastel-Daneshgar mu
 sical instrument collection\, with a focus on strings\, flutes\, and percu
 ssion from across Asia\, the Middle East\, and North Africa. Crafted from 
 wood\, snakeskin\, horsehair\, lambskin\, camel bone\, and other unique ma
 terials\, these instruments provide a compelling history of cultural and a
 rtistic exchanges that have led to the myriad musical sounds and styles th
 at permeate the world today. The opening event will feature remarks from t
 he collection owner\, Shahyar Daneshgar\, and will feature musical clips p
 rovided by the Lotus World Music & Arts Festival.\nShahyar Daneshgar is a 
 lifelong musician\, scholar\, and cultural ambassador whose passion for tr
 aditional music has shaped more than three decades of collecting\, teachin
 g\, and performing. Born into a large musical family\, he studied at the T
 ehran Conservatory of Music before pursuing academic work in Germany and l
 ater at Indiana University\, where he earned his Ph.D. in Central Eurasian
  Studies in 1995. He taught at Indiana University until his retirement in 
 2024. He is founder and director of the Bloomington Silk Road Ensemble.\nT
 he exhibition is free and runs from September 25\, 2026\, to September 202
 7.\nThe mission of the McCalla Center is to provide accessible exhibition 
 and educational experiences\, creating opportunities for students\, facult
 y\, and the community to engage with collections\, IU-generated creative a
 ctivity\, and community projects in meaningful and innovative ways.
GEO:39.1709246;-86.527549
LOCATION:McCalla Center\, McCalla Center\, 525 E. 9th Street\, Bloomington
 \, IN 47408
URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/event/treasures-of-the-silk-road/5960
 4/
X-SOURCE:Visit Bloomington
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.visitbloomington.com/events/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
